{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '245,282p;590,625p;1240,1290p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '290,315p;405,440p;590,607p;625,700p;1340,1364p;1520,1532p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc | sed -n '285,340p;395,430p;470,490p;680,760p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '245,282p;590,625p;1240,1290p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '290,315p;405,440p;590,607p;625,700p;1340,1364p;1520,1532p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc | sed -n '285,340p;395,430p;470,490p;680,760p'\"","aggregated_output":"   245\trestoration, and scheduling outside the stamp. UCF therefore versions the\n   246\t**complete execution contract**: the stamp names one published revision of\n   247\t\n   248\t- the tuple grammar of §7.3.2 — the mnemonic set, each mnemonic's tuple\n   249\t  arity and operand kinds, and the saturation/defaults table — plus the\n   250\t  §2.6 well-formedness rules and the resolved-interpretation\n   251\t  (last-value-wins) rule;\n   252\t- the §2.4 opcode table and the §4.2 transitions;\n   253\t- the resolution precedence of `resolve-var` (env → store → primitives →\n   254\t  modules);\n   255\t- the effect→outcome mapping of §3.3 and `engine/handle-effect`;\n   256\t- call restoration and the scheduler semantics of §3.5 — wait-entry shapes,\n   257\t  `check-wait-set`'s round order, `semantic-restore`.\n   258\t\n   259\t```clojure\n   260\t:yin.k/contract {:yin.code/contract \"v2\"    ; the revision above, by name\n   261\t                 :yin.k/version    0}       ; this envelope's own version\n   262\t```\n   263\t\n   264\tA resumer whose loader implements a different revision does not guess: it\n   265\treturns `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (§7.9) *before* lowering. Adding an opcode,\n   266\tchanging a transition, or changing the resolution order is a new revision; a\n   267\tresumer on the old one cannot run code that observes the difference, and the\n   268\tstamp is how it finds out before lowering rather than at an unknown integer\n   269\tin `case`. Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a published\n   270\tstatement in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4, never by an engine's own judgment.\n   271\tBecause interpretation is part of identity, an engine's code index is keyed\n   272\t**per stamp revision**: lookup is `(get-in index [stamp address])`, and the\n   273\tsame address under different stamps is a different image the same way the\n   274\tsame datoms under different loaders are a different program.\n   275\t\n   276\t### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\n   277\t\n   278\tEvery place the local record says `:segment <id>` — the control, each\n   279\t`:return` frame, each effect frame, each closure's `:segment` — the UCF frame\n   280\tsays `:yin.k/segment <address>`. Entry pcs and frame pcs are unchanged: a pc\n   281\tis meaningful relative to the resolved stream and is stable under everything\n   282\t§7.3.2 normalizes away.\n   590\tref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\n   591\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\n   592\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\n   593\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\n   594\t\n   595\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\n   596\t\n   597\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\n   598\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\n   599\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\n   600\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\n   601\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\n   602\t\n   603\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\n   604\t  ambiguous;\n   605\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\n   606\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\n   607\t  behavior;\n   608\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\n   609\t\n   610\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\n   611\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\n   612\t\n   613\t```clojure\n   614\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\n   615\t:yin.k/primitives {+\n   616\t                   {:yin.k/profile :yin.k.pp/sha256-…   ; content address of the profile record\n   617\t                    :yin.k/class    :pure               ; :pure | :effectful | :host\n   618\t                    :yin.k/arities  [2]\n   619\t                    :yin.k/effects  #{}                 ; for :effectful — declared effect kinds\n   620\t                    :yin.k/host-state :none}}           ; :none, or refused\n   621\t```\n   622\t\n   623\tThe profile record — `{name class arities effects host-state}` under a\n   624\tcontent address — is what a name must be *checked against*, not merely\n   625\tpresent as. Rules:\n  1240\tis closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries the data needed to act on it.\n  1241\t\n  1242\t| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n  1243\t|---|---|---|\n  1244\t| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n  1245\t| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n  1246\t| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\n  1247\t| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\n  1248\t| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\n  1249\t| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\n  1250\t| `:yin.k/undecodable` | lower | `:yin.k/path`, the structural defect — malformed markers, missing or cyclic refs, malformed-but-correctly-hashed bodies |\n  1251\t| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing`, `:yin.k/discovery` — includes attachment failures, cursor profiles, and an unreachable arbitration medium |\n  1252\t| `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the proposal — a step status while a proposal is unanswered, bounded by nothing (`dao.lease.md`) |\n  1253\t| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the lease state observed |\n  1254\t\n  1255\tTerminal DaoStream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\n  1256\t`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`, …) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF\n  1257\toutcome under their own key `:dao.stream/outcome`, exactly as the transport\n  1258\tproduced them; UCF does not rename them, nest them under private keys, or\n  1259\ttranslate between them. FFI requests and responses inside a value use the\n  1260\treference machine's versioned envelope, `dao.stream.apply` —\n  1261\t`:dao.stream.apply/id`, `/op`, `/args`, `/ok`, `/error` — never the\n  1262\tdeleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\n  1263\tcleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\n  1264\t(§7.8 step 7).\n  1265\t\n  1266\t## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n  1267\t\n  1268\t| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n  1269\t|---|---|\n  1270\t| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires` — *as computed by the declared fixed point, with its completeness stated* (`:yin.k/discovery`); the store travels as an explicit slice restored into an isolated store; fresh-name state and parked records travel explicitly; custody is facts on a named, possessed medium. |\n  1271\t| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links; the exporting state is an observed data state, never a notification. |\n  1272\t| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound; an unanswered proposal is a step status, not a wait. |\n  1273\t| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; occurrence identity separates content variants from the one leased computation; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations; the isolated execution store shares nothing with the resumer's tasks. |\n  1274\t| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, discover, arbitrate, load, restore, execute are separate functions with data between them. The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery. |\n  1275\t| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; cursor aliasing is an explicit cell graph, not inferred from positions; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed vector; nothing is resolved by pointer; foreign engines reconstruct dynamic state from the frame, never from layouts they assume. |\n  1276\t\n  1277\tAnd the axioms — each claim conditioned on the obligation it depends on: the\n  1278\tcontinuation is a stream value (1); its meaning is supplied by the\n  1279\tresumer's loader and contract stamp, not carried in it (2); its code and its\n  1280\tstate are datoms under `:yin.code/*` and `:yin.k/*` (3); and it pauses,\n  1281\ttravels, and resumes anywhere that **holds what it declares, satisfies the\n  1282\tdeclared cursor profiles, and obtains custody where the policy demands it**\n  1283\t(4) — the unconditional form of that sentence was a claim the unresolved\n  1284\tstate closure, custody enforcement, and effect admission had not earned.\n  1285\t\n  1286\t## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n  1287\t\n  1288\tThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\n  1289\tclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\n  1290\tthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n   290\t  ;;           :yin.ast/code; a pc for the vector formats\n   291\t  ```\n   292\t\n   293\t  `:conditional?` is true when any `:if` branch, jump target range, or\n   294\t  lambda body encloses the definition; `:in-body?` is true when a\n   295\t  lambda body encloses the occurrence; an `:applications-fn` record is\n   296\t  one **application site**: a position at which this image may apply a\n   297\t  closure it creates -- a direct call site, a call of an imported\n   298\t  name, or a call of a primitive that receives a function argument,\n   299\t  because the callee may apply what it is handed. Dominance between\n   300\t  two positions is decided by the linker from these fields and the\n   301\t  format's order (step 5a). For the AST record all three scanners are\n   302\t  Datalog over the rows: `yin.vm/free-names` today returns a symbol\n   303\t  set, and is extended (or wrapped by an occurrence-scoped query over\n   304\t  the same rules) to return each occurrence's path; the definitions\n   305\t  scanner is the store-key query behind `yin.vm/ast-requirements`\n   306\t  with the binding row's path and its enclosing tags; the application\n   307\t  scanner is the application-row query over the same relation. For\n   308\t  the vector formats all three are operand scans by pc (`:var`\n   309\t  against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the `yin/def` call\n   310\t  sites, the call opcodes for applications) with conditional target\n   311\t  ranges taken from the `:jump-if` and body operands. A format whose\n   312\t  scanner returns positions as `nil` is admitted and falls under the\n   313\t  conservative degradation of step 5a: every occurrence is retained\n   314\t  as an obligation, and with no application positions no occurrence\n   315\t  inside a lambda body is discharged at all.\n   405\t   vector, over the vector; for a register image, including the\n   406\t   ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A validator that throws is\n   407\t   `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:validator-refused`.\n   408\t5. **Verify dependency closure.** Two halves.\n   409\t   - *5a, at the linker: obligations.* Two scans run over the verified\n   410\t     value. `:obligations-fn` yields the free-name occurrences, each\n   411\t     with its position: for a tree, the occurrence path and whether it\n   412\t     lies inside a `:lambda` body; for a vector, the pc and whether it\n   413\t     lies in a body rather than the main sequence. `:definitions-fn`\n   414\t     yields the **definition set** with positions: every constant store\n   415\t     key the image binds at module level (the section 7.7 store-key\n   416\t     query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`: `:vm/store-put` rows,\n   417\t     `:store-put` operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key),\n   418\t     each with the position of the binding in the main sequence.\n   419\t     A definition discharges an occurrence by **defined-before-use\n   420\t     under control-flow dominance**, not by subtraction of names and\n   421\t     not by source order alone. Source order says a definition is\n   422\t     written earlier; dominance says it has executed by the time the\n   423\t     occurrence runs. Only the second discharges, and where dominance\n   424\t     cannot be established the obligation is **retained**, never\n   425\t     dropped:\n   426\t     - A definition is *unconditional* when it sits in the main\n   427\t       sequence at top level or inside a `:do` (for a tree) or at a pc\n   428\t       in the main sequence outside every conditional target range\n   429\t       (for a vector); it is *conditional* when any `:if` branch, any\n   430\t       jump target, or any lambda body encloses it. A conditional\n   431\t       definition discharges nothing statically: the linker cannot know\n   432\t       the branch was taken, so an occurrence that depends on it keeps\n   433\t       its obligation and is checked at the receiver under 5b exactly\n   434\t       as an undefined name would be, and the manifest must declare it\n   435\t       or the link is `:undeclared-free`. This is conservative by\n   436\t       design: a module that defines a name only in one branch and\n   437\t       reads it afterwards must declare what the name means when the\n   438\t       branch is not taken.\n   439\t     - An occurrence in the main sequence (outside every lambda body)\n   440\t       is discharged only by an unconditional definition of that name\n   590\t\n   591\tThree records bind existing code; the AST record's free-name scanner is\n   592\tthe section 4.5 query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, implemented as\n   593\t`yin.vm/free-names`.\n   594\t\n   595\t### 5.1 `:yin.ast/code` (the AST walker)\n   596\t\n   597\t```clojure\n   598\t{:format              :yin.ast/code\n   599\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   600\t :identity-fn         (fn [body] (jing/segment-key body))\n   601\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [root-id body] (jing/segment-matches? root-id\n   602\t                                                                  body))\n   603\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [body] (row-local-defect body))\n   604\t :validate-fn         (fn [tree] (yin.vm/validate-rows tree))\n   605\t :obligations-fn      (fn [tree] (free-name-occurrences tree))\n   606\t :definitions-fn      (fn [tree] (definition-occurrences tree))\n   607\t :applications-fn     (fn [tree] (application-sites tree))\n   625\teach occurrence's path and `:in-body?`; `definition-occurrences` is\n   626\tthe store-key query carrying each binding row's path and\n   627\t`:conditional?`; `application-sites` is the application-row query.\n   628\tLoading is `yin.vm.ast-walker/vm-load-rows`.\n   629\t\n   630\t### 5.2 `:yin.semantic/code` (the semantic VM)\n   631\t\n   632\t```clojure\n   633\t{:format              :yin.semantic/code\n   634\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   635\t :identity-fn         jing/segment-key\n   636\t :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?\n   637\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   638\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   639\t :obligations-fn      semantic-free-names\n   640\t :definitions-fn      semantic-definitions\n   641\t :applications-fn     semantic-application-sites\n   642\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   643\t```\n   644\t\n   645\tPayload: the canonical positional instruction tuple vector (UCF section\n   646\t7.3.2), stored as the exact vector at its own `segment-key`; nothing\n   647\telse may hash there (UCF section 7.3.4). The identity is that address.\n   648\t`well-formed-vector?` is the section 7.5 grammar. `semantic-free-names`\n   649\tscans `:var` operands not bound by an enclosing closure's params, the\n   650\t`$code` query of section 7.7; `semantic-application-sites` scans the\n   651\tcall opcodes, including calls of imported names and of primitives\n   652\tthat receive a function argument; all three return the section 4.1\n   653\trecords. The linker module defines them, as B6 defined the two de\n   654\tBruijn scanners. The contract stamp is UCF's\n   655\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v2\"}`; a link request for this format carries the\n   656\tstamp, and the receiver's index is keyed per stamp (UCF section 7.3.3).\n   657\tLoading is `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`, which writes the address to the\n   658\timage and the alias column.\n   659\t\n   660\t### 5.3 `:yin.debruijn.code` (the stack VM)\n   661\t\n   662\t```clojure\n   663\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code\n   664\t :contract            \"b1\"\n   665\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   666\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [H v] (= H (yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash v)))\n   667\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   668\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   669\t :obligations-fn      stack-free-names\n   670\t :definitions-fn      stack-definitions\n   671\t :applications-fn     stack-application-sites\n   672\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   673\t```\n   674\t\n   675\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.1 in substance. The identity H is\n   676\t`image-hash` over the descriptor hash (which embeds the lowering contract\n   677\tversion) and the canonical vector; the `=` here compares two values of\n   678\tone contract-pinned hash and is not a Class 3 site. Free names are\n   679\t`:load-free` operands; application sites are `:call` operands,\n   680\tincluding calls of imported names and function-argument primitives.\n   681\tLoading is `yin.vm.debruijn.stack/load-image`;\n   682\t`yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` shows the relocation a second image\n   683\tneeds beside a held one.\n   684\t\n   685\t### 5.4 `:yin.debruijn.register` (the register VM)\n   686\t\n   687\t```clojure\n   688\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.register\n   689\t :contract            \"r1\"\n   690\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   691\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [R v]\n   692\t                        (= R (yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   693\t                               v)))\n   694\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   695\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   696\t :obligations-fn      register-free-names\n   697\t :definitions-fn      register-definitions\n   698\t :applications-fn     register-application-sites\n   699\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   700\t```\n  1340\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\n  1341\t    closure.\n  1342\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\n  1343\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\n  1344\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\n  1345\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\n  1346\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\n  1347\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\n  1348\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\n  1349\t    7.6.2's \"there is no merge\" rule at the module grain -- a\n  1350\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\n  1351\t    the module's value.\n  1352\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\n  1353\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\n  1354\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\n  1355\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\n  1356\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\n  1357\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\n  1358\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it with **no ambient fallback**:\n  1359\t    a key the module store lacks is absent, exactly as a top-level\n  1360\t    read of an absent key is; engine resources are unreachable\n  1361\t    either way, by the private-resource rule below. The context\n  1362\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\n  1363\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\n  1364\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\n  1520\taddress. It replaces the `{sym fn}` dictionary entry.\n  1521\t\n  1522\t```clojure\n  1523\t{:yin.module/name        'my.lib\n  1524\t :yin.module/schema      1                   ; this container's shape\n  1525\t :yin.module/contracts   {:yin.ast/code          \"v2\"   ; per format\n  1526\t                          :yin.semantic/code     \"v2\"\n  1527\t                          :yin.debruijn.code     \"b1\"\n  1528\t                          :yin.debruijn.register \"r1\"}\n  1529\t :yin.module/tree        :segment/...        ; the one canonical tree\n  1530\t :yin.module/derivations {:yin.semantic/code     :segment/...  ; record\n  1531\t                          :yin.debruijn.code     :segment/...  ; record\n  1532\t                          :yin.debruijn.register :segment/...} ; record\n   285\t  [d u]\n   286\t  (neg? (path-order d u)))\n   287\t\n   288\t\n   289\t(def ^:private tree-occurrence-query\n   290\t  \"`vm/free-names`' free-name query over the row and occurrence\n   291\t   relations, extended to return each occurrence's path (section 4.1):\n   292\t   the same rules, one more find column.\"\n   293\t  '[:find ?name ?path\n   294\t    :in $ $occ % ?root\n   295\t    :where\n   296\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   297\t    [?v :variable ?name]\n   298\t    (not (occ-bound? ?root ?path ?name))])\n   299\t\n   300\t\n   301\t(def ^:private tree-definition-query\n   302\t  \"The definition query behind `vm/ast-requirements` (section 4.1): the\n   303\t   `:vm/store-put` rows and the constant-key `yin/def` applications the\n   304\t   macro expander produces (an application of the `yin/def` variable\n   305\t   with exactly two operands, the first a literal), each binding joined\n   306\t   with its binding row's occurrence path -- the definitions scanner\n   307\t   extends an application's path one step past its operands, to the\n   308\t   invocation position (see `tree-definition-occurrences`).\"\n   309\t  '[:find ?key ?path\n   310\t    :in $ $occ ?root\n   311\t    :where\n   312\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   313\t    (or-join [?v ?key]\n   314\t             [?v :vm/store-put ?key _]\n   315\t             (and [?v :application ?op ?operands _]\n   316\t                  [?op :variable yin/def]\n   317\t                  [(count ?operands) 2]\n   318\t                  [(nth ?operands 0) ?name-id]\n   319\t                  [?name-id :literal ?key]))])\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\t(def ^:private tree-application-query\n   323\t  \"The application-row query over the same relation (section 4.1): one\n   324\t   application site per `:application` row, carried with the row's\n   325\t   operand count -- the site is recorded where the application happens,\n   326\t   which is only after the operands have run.\"\n   327\t  '[:find ?path ?n\n   328\t    :in $ $occ ?root\n   329\t    :where\n   330\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   331\t    [?v :application _ ?operands _]\n   332\t    [(count ?operands) ?n]])\n   333\t\n   334\t\n   335\t(defn- tree-row-at\n   336\t  \"The row `path` names from the tree's root, nil when the path leaves\n   337\t   the tree. A step is a `:node` slot's row position or a `[position i]`\n   338\t   pair into a `:nodes` slot's vector (section 6.1).\"\n   339\t  [{:keys [root rows]} path]\n   340\t  (loop [id root, steps (seq path)]\n   395\t                (let [[in-body? _] (tree-enclosure tree path)]\n   396\t                  {:name name, :at [root path], :in-body? in-body?})))\n   397\t         (sort-by (comp second :at) path-order)\n   398\t         vec)))\n   399\t\n   400\t\n   401\t(def ^:private tree-yin-def-application-query\n   402\t  \"Every application of the `yin/def` variable, with its operand ids\n   403\t   (section 4.1): the sites whose key the definition query may not have\n   404\t   read as a constant.\"\n   405\t  '[:find ?path ?operands\n   406\t    :in $ $occ ?root\n   407\t    :where\n   408\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   409\t    [?v :application ?op ?operands _]\n   410\t    [?op :variable yin/def]])\n   411\t\n   412\t\n   413\t(defn- computed-yin-def-write?\n   414\t  \"Whether the module has a `yin/def` application whose key is not\n   415\t   statically a constant: any shape but two operands, the first a\n   416\t   literal, may write a name that evaluates to `yin/def`.\"\n   417\t  [{:keys [rows]} db occ root]\n   418\t  (boolean\n   419\t    (some (fn [[_ operands]]\n   420\t            (not (and (= 2 (count operands))\n   421\t                      (= :literal\n   422\t                         (nth (get rows (nth operands 0)) 1)))))\n   423\t          (query/collect\n   424\t            (query/q tree-yin-def-application-query db occ root)))))\n   425\t\n   426\t\n   427\t(defn- tree-definition-occurrences\n   428\t  \"Every constant store key the tree binds at module level as a section\n   429\t   4.1 record `{:name key :at [root path] :conditional? b}`: the\n   430\t   store-key query behind `vm/ast-requirements` (`:vm/store-put` rows\n   470\t                            :yin-def? yin-def?,\n   471\t                            :conditional? conditional?})))\n   472\t                  (sort-by (comp second :at) path-order)\n   473\t                  vec)]\n   474\t    (->> (if (or (some (fn [d] (= 'yin/def (:name d))) defs)\n   475\t                 (computed-yin-def-write? tree db occ root))\n   476\t           (filterv (complement :yin-def?) defs)\n   477\t           defs)\n   478\t         (mapv (fn [d] (dissoc d :yin-def?))))))\n   479\t\n   480\t\n   481\t(defn- tree-application-sites\n   482\t  \"Every application row of the tree as a section 4.1 record `{:at\n   483\t   [root path]}` -- one application site at its invocation position: the\n   484\t   row's own path extended one step past its operands, the position at\n   485\t   which the walker applies the operator after evaluating them\n   486\t   (section 4.1), so a definition inside an operand dominates an\n   487\t   application of the enclosing form.\"\n   488\t  [tree]\n   489\t  (let [{:keys [root db occ]} (tree-rows tree)]\n   490\t    (->> (query/collect\n   680\t(def stack-format\n   681\t  \"The `:yin.debruijn.code` format record: stack images identified by H\n   682\t   under the \\\"b1\\\" contract, one canonical vector per image. Free names\n   683\t   are `:load-free` operands, definitions `:store-put` operands,\n   684\t   application sites the call opcodes -- all position-bearing by pc\n   685\t   (section 5.3).\"\n   686\t  {:format              :yin.debruijn.code,\n   687\t   :contract            \"b1\",\n   688\t   :identity-fn         debruijn-code/image-hash,\n   689\t   :identity-matches-fn (fn [H v] (= H (debruijn-code/image-hash v))),\n   690\t   :row-defect-fn       debruijn-code/image-defect,\n   691\t   :validate-fn         debruijn-code/image-defect,\n   692\t   :obligations-fn      stack-free-occurrences,\n   693\t   :definitions-fn      vector-definition-occurrences,\n   694\t   :applications-fn     vector-application-sites,\n   695\t   :parts-fn            (constantly nil)})\n   696\t\n   697\t\n   698\t(def register-format\n   699\t  \"The `:yin.debruijn.register` format record: register images identified\n   700\t   by R under the \\\"r1\\\" contract, one image map per image. Free names\n   701\t   are `[:load-free rd name]` operands across all bodies, definitions\n   702\t   the `:store-put` keys, application sites the call opcodes\n   703\t   (section 5.4).\"\n   704\t  {:format              :yin.debruijn.register,\n   705\t   :contract            \"r1\",\n   706\t   :identity-fn         debruijn-register-code/register-hash,\n   707\t   :identity-matches-fn (fn [R v]\n   708\t                          (= R (debruijn-register-code/register-hash v))),\n   709\t   :row-defect-fn       debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect,\n   710\t   :validate-fn         debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect,\n   711\t   :obligations-fn      register-free-occurrences,\n   712\t   :definitions-fn      register-definition-occurrences,\n   713\t   :applications-fn     register-application-sites,\n   714\t   :parts-fn            (constantly nil)})\n   715\t\n   716\t\n   717\t(def semantic-format\n   718\t  \"The `:yin.semantic/code` format record: the canonical positional\n   719\t   instruction vector stored as the exact payload at its own\n   720\t   `segment-key` (UCF section 7.3.4), under the \\\"v2\\\" contract. Identity\n   721\t   and address are one preimage; step 3 still runs, because it is the\n   722\t   only check that the index did not point the identity at a different,\n   723\t   correctly stored payload (section 4.2). Free names are `:var`\n   724\t   operands, definitions `:store-put` operands, application sites the\n   725\t   call opcodes (section 5.2).\"\n   726\t  {:format              :yin.semantic/code,\n   727\t   :contract            \"v2\",\n   728\t   :identity-fn         jing/segment-key,\n   729\t   :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?,\n   730\t   :row-defect-fn       code/well-formed-vector?,\n   731\t   :validate-fn         code/well-formed-vector?,\n   732\t   :obligations-fn      semantic-free-occurrences,\n   733\t   :definitions-fn      vector-definition-occurrences,\n   734\t   :applications-fn     vector-application-sites,\n   735\t   :parts-fn            (constantly nil)})\n   736\t\n   737\t\n   738\t(def ast-format\n   739\t  \"The `:yin.ast/code` format record: one row body `[tag & slots]` per\n   740\t   address (D3), the root row's id the tree's identity, under the \\\"v2\\\"\n   741\t   contract. Step 2 is a bounded worklist over the grammar's\n   742\t   `node`/`nodes` slots; the whole tree is assembled for step 4 by\n   743\t   prepending each part's address, the shape `validate-rows` takes. All\n   744\t   three scanners are Datalog over the rows, position-bearing by\n   745\t   occurrence path (section 5.1).\"\n   746\t  {:format              :yin.ast/code,\n   747\t   :contract            \"v2\",\n   748\t   :identity-fn         jing/segment-key,\n   749\t   :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?,\n   750\t   :row-defect-fn       row-local-defect,\n   751\t   :validate-fn         vm/validate-rows,\n   752\t   :obligations-fn      tree-free-name-occurrences,\n   753\t   :definitions-fn      tree-definition-occurrences,\n   754\t   :applications-fn     tree-application-sites,\n   755\t   :parts-fn            body-child-ids})\n   756\t\n   757\t\n   758\t;; =============================================================================\n   759\t;; The H and R indexes (section 6)\n   760\t;; =============================================================================\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
