\t                :stream-cursor #{:stream/cursor},\n  1425\t                :stream-next #{:stream/next},\n  1426\t                :stream-close #{:stream/close},\n  1427\t                :ffi-call #{},\n  1428\t                :park #{},\n  1429\t                :resume #{},\n  1430\t                :gensym #{},\n  1431\t                :store-get #{},\n  1432\t                :store-put #{},\n  1433\t                :current-continuation #{},\n  1434\t                :const #{}, :var #{}, :closure #{}, :push #{}, :call #{},\n  1435\t                :return #{}, :jump #{}, :branch-false #{}, :halt #{}}}})\n  1475\t   unverified there; `free-names` over `$ast`/`$occ` is the shipped\n  1476\t   extraction.\"\n  1477\t  {:store-keys '[:find ?key :in $code\n  1478\t                 :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]\n  1479\t                            [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])],\n  1480\t   :ffi-ops '[:find ?op :in $code\n  1481\t              :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]],\n  1482\t   :parked-ids '[:find ?pid :in $code\n  1483\t                 :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]],\n  1484\t   :effect-raisers '[:find ?mn :in $code\n  1485\t                     :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]\n  1486\t                     [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor\n  1487\t                                   :stream-next :stream-close :ffi-call}\n  1488\t                                 ?mn)]]})\n  1489\t\n  1490\t\n  1491\t(defn- requirements\n  1492\t  \"The §7.7 syntactic requirement set of one relation under contract\n  1493\t   `\\\"v2\\\"`: the three value queries as extracted, and the effect-raising\n  1494\t   tags/mnemonics normalized through the footprint table's `dir` direction\n  1495\t   before any union, so the union is over one vocabulary (§7.7.1).\"\n  1496\t  [db queries dir]\n  1497\t  (let [table (get-in footprint-table [\"v2\" dir])]\n  1498\t    {:store-keys (extract (:store-keys queries) db),\n  1499\t     :ffi-ops (extract (:ffi-ops queries) db),\n  1500\t     :parked-ids (extract (:parked-ids queries) db),\n  1501\t     :effects (into #{} (mapcat table) (extract (:effect-raisers queries) db))}))\n  1502\t\n  1503\t\n  1504\t(defn ast-requirements\n  1505\t  \"§7.7's syntactic requirement set from a tree relation `$ast` — a\n  1506\t   `query/relation` over the flat rows of the reachable trees (e.g.\n  1507\t   `(vals (:rows (ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))`, unioned across trees):\n  1508\t   store keys, FFI ops, parked ids, and effect identifiers, effects\n  1509\t   already normalized by the footprint table. These are the fields of\n  1510\t   `:yin.k/requires` the syntactic queries fill; free names are\n  1511\t   `free-names`, and the value/name/module parts of UCF §7.6.1's fixed\n  1512\t   point remain the walk that consumes this.\"\n  1513\t  [db]\n  1514\t  (requirements db ast-extraction-queries :tags))\n  1515\t\n   225\t**Saturation and defaults.** Two operand attributes fall back to a default\n   226\twhen absent rather than failing to decode: `:gensym`'s `:yin.code/prefix`\n   227\tdefaults to `\"id\"`; `:stream-make`'s `:yin.code/buffer` defaults to\n   228\t`yin.vm/default-stream-capacity`. Every other operand attribute in the\n   229\t§2.4 table is required by its op — the decoder does not guess at it.\n   230\t\n   231\t**The `\"v2\"` execution contract, published.** `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n   232\t§7.11 names a contract-revision-publication blocker for `:yin.code/contract\n   233\t\"v2\"`: a revision history naming the mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and\n   234\toperand kinds, the saturation/defaults table, the opcode table and\n   235\ttransitions, resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map,\n   236\tand scheduler semantics, all in this section. All seven are already written,\n   237\there or by direct citation:\n   238\t\n   239\t- **Mnemonic set**: the `:yin.code/op` column of §2.4's table, 21 values,\n   240\t  matching `yin.vm.code/mnemonics`.\n   241\t- **Per-mnemonic arity and operand kinds**: §2.4's \"Operand attributes\"\n   242\t  column; enforced by §2.6 rule 2 (`:instruction-shape`) and rule 5\n   243\t  (`:dangling-target`, for the three ref-bearing ops).\n   244\t- **Saturation/defaults**: the paragraph directly above.\n   245\t- **Opcode table and transitions**: the \"Opcode integers\" paragraph above\n   246\t  for the mnemonic→integer mapping; §4.2 for the transition equations.\n   247\t- **Resolution rule**: §4.2's $\\rho$ = `engine/resolve-var`,\n   248\t  env → store → primitives → module registry (`ast.md` Part 2 gives the\n   249\t  same order for the walker — one resolution rule, two evaluators).\n   250\t- **Last-value-wins rule**: a repeated single-valued instruction attribute\n   251\t  keeps its last value in datom order, exactly as `code.cljc`'s\n   252\t  `index-batch` and `yin.vm/index-datoms` both implement it.\n   253\t- **Effect outcome map**: §3.3's table.\n   254\t- **Scheduler semantics**: §3.5.\n   255\t\n   256\tThis is the whole `\"v2\"` contract by reference, not a duplicate of it —\n   257\teach piece has exactly one home, and this note is that home's index.\n   258\t\n   259\t### 2.5 Constants and literals\n   260\t\n   325\t`lower-stack` consumes resolved tuples and their side table, calls\n   326\t`validate-resolved` first and refuses on its diagnostic, then emits the\n   327\tcanonical positional `:yin.debruijn.code/*` image (section 2) plus the\n   328\tpc-keyed diagnostic side table the lift needs. It cannot call\n   329\t`yin.vm.linearize/lower`, which reads `:yin/name` and `:yin/params`. It\n   330\ttherefore reproduces the named linearizer's flattening itself: the same\n   331\trecursion in evaluation order, the same `:push` after operator and after\n   332\teach operand, `:call` with argc and the copied `:yin/tail?`, `if` as\n   333\t`branch-false`, `jump`, and two labels, `:halt` after the root, lambda\n   334\tbodies out of line in discovery order each ending in `:return`, and labels\n   335\tresolved to pcs in a second pass. Like `lower`, it expands occurrences\n   336\tpositionally: a resolved record referenced twice is emitted twice, at two\n   337\tpcs; the resolver's occurrence memo never changes emitted bytes. A\n   338\t`:variable` emits `[:load-bound depth position]` or `[:load-free name]`\n   339\tstraight from its resolved attributes; a `:lambda` emits `[:closure arity\n   340\tbody-pc]`. Every other node emits the carried mnemonic unchanged.\n   341\t\n   342\tLayout equality with the named linearizer no longer holds by construction.\n   343\tIt is a required structural-comparison test: for every corpus program the\n   344\tcanonical vector of `(lower x)` and `(lower-stack (resolve x))` have the\n   345\tsame length and the same mnemonic at every pc, and differ only at `:var`\n   346\tversus `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands and at `:closure`'s parameter\n   347\tversus arity operand. A layout change in `yin.vm.linearize` must fail this\n   367\tvalues for a corpus on JVM, CLJS, and CLJD.\n   368\t\n   369\t### 4.4 Instruction mapping\n   370\t\n   371\tThe register instruction set is a positional form of the B1 table:\n   372\t\n   373\t    :const        [op rd value]\n   374\t    :load-bound   [op rd depth position]\n   375\t    :load-free    [op rd name]\n   376\t    :closure      [op rd arity body-pc]\n   377\t    :move         [op rd rs]\n   378\t    :call         [op rd fn-reg arg-regs tail? live]\n   379\t    :branch-false [op cond-reg target]\n   380\t    :jump         [op target]\n   381\t    :return       [op value-reg]\n   382\t    :halt         [op value-reg]\n   383\t    :store-get    [op rd key]\n   384\t    :store-put    [op rd key value]\n   385\t    :gensym       [op rd prefix]\n   386\t    :stream-make  [op rd capacity]\n   387\t    :stream-put   [op rd stream-reg value-reg live]\n   388\t    :stream-cursor [op rd stream-reg]\n   389\t    :stream-next  [op rd cursor-reg live]\n   565\tand analysis in one phase; it does not introduce a second liveness pass.\n   566\t\n   567\t### 4.6 R2 lowering rules\n   568\t\n   569\tR2 completes every node type R1 deferred. It extends the existing\n   570\ttarget-register-passing walk; it does not introduce an effect AST or a\n   571\tsecond allocator.\n   572\t\n   573\t    resolved node                 lowering\n   574\t    :vm/store-get                 [:store-get rd key]\n   575\t    :vm/store-put                 [:store-put rd key value]\n   576\t    :vm/gensym                    [:gensym rd prefix]\n   577\t    :stream/make                  [:stream-make rd buffer]\n   578\t    :stream/put                   lower target, then value;\n   579\t                                  [:stream-put rd rt rv live]\n   580\t    :stream/cursor                lower source;\n   581\t                                  [:stream-cursor rd rs]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:31:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:31:24 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nRule R is a sound direction for making definitions unshadowable, but Fable’s enforcement plan is not yet complete. This was a read-only review; no suites were rerun.\n\nFactual claims:\n\n- **PARTLY — `def` is already a special form.** The Yang frontend recognizes `def` before ordinary variable lookup, but lowers it to an ordinary application of `yin/def`; the VM does not yet preserve its special-form meaning (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:132-160`).\n- **TRUE — `yin/def` is an ordinary primitive today.** Its registry entry returns a `:vm/store-put` effect (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:194-197`).\n- **TRUE, narrowly — all four engines use the shared variable resolver.** The walker, semantic, stack, and register engines call `engine/resolve-var`, whose order is environment → store → primitives → modules (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:379`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:263-267`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:386-390`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:390-395`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:54-73`). Direct store instructions do not use it.\n- **FALSE as stated — only primitives raise effects, and all other store keys are syntactically visible.** Built-in store instructions write directly, and a composition-supplied host function can return an effect with a computed key; effect dispatch accepts it (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:419-438`, `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:143-150`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:77-80`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:510-516`). The literal-key claim holds for the portable built-in store instructions, not every executable path.\n\nActionable findings:\n\nP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:48` | Refusing newly harvested `yin/def` macros does not reject one already present in the expander’s incoming store. Expansion consults that store before expanding an application, so it can rewrite a definition before linker validation (`src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:973-980`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1105-1109`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1170-1172`). | Refuse a reserved macro binding at expander-context admission, including restored stores, and test a prior-batch binding.\n\nP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:23-24` | “Every image reaching any engine has passed” linker validation is false: the walker also accepts datom input directly, and raw AST execution has variable, store-get, store-put, and store-update paths (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:709-721`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:377-438`). A row-local check also cannot classify a shared `yin/def` variable row by its use site; the linker’s AST scans are occurrence-path based (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:289-319`). | Specify reserved-name checks at every direct admission/evaluation path and an occurrence-aware whole-tree check. Define which violations are rejected at load time versus transition time.\n\nP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:14-15` | Removing the named primitive does not establish the proposed “store never contains `yin/def`” invariant: an allowed host callable can emit `{:effect :vm/store-put :key 'yin/def ...}`, and the central dispatcher currently accepts that key (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:188-196`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:510-516`). This would **not** shadow a properly implemented syntactic definition, but it invalidates Fable’s stronger store and discovery claims. | Guard the reserved key in effect dispatch, direct store operations, and supplied initial stores; retain incomplete-discovery treatment for dynamic host effects.\n\nP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:38-46` | Fable explicitly calls for stack/register revisions but not new AST and semantic contracts. Rule R changes AST application interpretation and adds a semantic opcode; UCF requires a new revision for changed transitions or opcodes, while the linker still advertises `\"v2\"`, `\"b1\"`, and `\"r1\"` (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:245-274`, `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:595-604`, `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:630-699`). | Version all four execution contracts, update format records and manifest/derivation stamps, and require old-contract images to be refused or handled by an explicitly compatible loader.\n\nP2 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:19` | “The symbol `yin/def` may occur only as operator” conflates a variable occurrence with quoted data. A literal symbol is data (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:140-143`); banning `(quote yin/def)` is unnecessary, while allowing it as a definition key is not. | State the rule by syntactic role: permit literal data, reject reserved binders and store keys, and validate the target key at any dynamic store-effect boundary.\n\n“Sound and complete for constant keys” is achievable **for admitted images under a single revised execution contract**: a valid `yin/def` application would always define its literal key, while the existing invocation position correctly places the write after its value operand (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:427-468`). It is not established by the present proposal across macro ingress, direct execution, and old-contract or foreign engines.\n\nA dedicated `:vm/define` node is not inherently contrary to “derive, don’t persist”: it would encode a distinct operation once, but would migrate the row grammar and content hashes (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:795-807`). Keeping the current application shape is reasonable if one shared recognizer governs validation and lowering and the walker has matching transition tests. Fable is right to reject primitives-first lookup: it would leave `yin/def` first-class and aliasable. Once Rule R is fully enforced, the round-3/4 shadow filters and `:yin-def?` bookkeeping can go (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:401-424`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:474-478`); constant-key recognition, invocation positioning, dominance, and the earlier fetch-bound fixes must stay (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:301-319`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:427-468`).\n\nLand a complete, independently gated Rule R change before M2 if two commits can each remain coherent. Do **not** use Fable’s validator-only M2 fallback: direct execution and dynamic host effects remain outside that proof (`collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69-75`). Update the contradictions Fable identified in UCF and the linker, and also the macro contract’s stored-macro lookup, the semantic/stack/register instruction contracts, and code-as-tuples’ assertion that the `yin/def` operator is free (`docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:373-396`, `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:231-257`, `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:325-340`, `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:369-385`, `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1828-1832`). Store-update discovery may remain deferred; the admission, macro, and contract gaps above cannot.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\nSign-off: DENIED"}}
